WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.207
Read the controller as a trajectory, not only as source code.

00:00:04.207 --> 00:00:10.322
Step one retrieves with the original query and gets a weak policy match.

00:00:10.322 --> 00:00:15.272
Step two grades that evidence at zero point five, below the threshold.

00:00:15.272 --> 00:00:19.330
Step three rewrites the query using better policy terms.

00:00:19.882 --> 00:00:24.744
Step four retrieves again and now finds the fourteen-day return rule.

00:00:24.744 --> 00:00:30.539
Step five grades the new evidence at one point zero and routes to generation.

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:35.690
Step six returns the answer, evidence, and trace, then stops.

00:00:36.142 --> 00:00:42.079
A trajectory is useful because it lets you explain the run without reading every line of code.

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:48.914
For a project demo, showing a short trajectory often communicates the architecture better than showing a giant code

00:00:48.914 --> 00:00:49.635
cell.
